@article{WOS:000353730000062,
 abstract = {Facebook (FB)(1) is a popular platform for interacting with others to
establish or maintain relationships. Compared to other interpersonal
exchanges, FB does not require in-person interactions. Therefore, FB may
represent an important social sphere for individuals with social,
anxiety disorder (SAD).(2) Examining the relationship between social
anxiety symptoms and FB activity could inform future research on the
benefits or consequences of FB use in SAD individuals. This study
examined the relationship between social anxiety symptoms and different
FB usage patterns. We also considered the role of brooding a known risk
factor for SAD. 75 nonclinical FB users completed questionnaires about
psychological symptoms, FB usage, and brooding. Greater social anxiety
symptoms were associated with spending more time on FB and passively
using FB (i.e., viewing other's profiles without interacting). Brooding
mediated the relationship between passive FB use and social anxiety
symptoms. An alternative model demonstrated that social anxiety symptoms
mediated the association between passive FB use and brooding. This study
was limited by its cross-sectional, self-report design. Future research
should assess FB use with objective, real-time data and use experimental
designs. Results have implications for the cognitive-behavioral model of
SAD. (C) 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.},
