@article{WOS:000417424100003,
 abstract = {This study examined using patterns of risk and warning-related
information by backpackers who develop their own travel risk
perceptions. It looked at the preferences of information sources and
usage levels among Israeli backpackers. Using a simulating approach,
interviewees were asked to report on their preference and usage patterns
in four stages of the travel consumption sequence - (a) prior to choice
of destination; (b) after choice of destination but before departure;
(c) during the trip, and (d) after returning home. Based on a sample of
467 former backpackers, the study revealed that they use a relatively
wide range of risk-related sources of information yet their preference
and usage levels in those sources vary as they moved from one
consumption stage to the other. The most innovative finding of this
study is that unlike previous findings there was a significant and
direct correlation between the level of risk-related information
consumption and the backpackers' level of risk perception in each of the
tourist product consumption stages. Further research directions derived
from these findings as well as management implications are provided.},
