@article{WOS:000445239600017,
 abstract = {Excessive alcohol consumption has been shown to increase HIV risk for
men who have sex with men (MSM) and compromise HIV prevention behaviors.
However, there is limited contextual understanding of alcohol use for
MSM in rural sub-Saharan African settings, which can inform and direct
HIV interventions. Applying an adaptation of PhotoVoice, we worked with
35 HIV-positive MSM who created photo-essays about alcohol and HIV in
Mpumalanga. A semi-structured protocol was used in focus group
discussions that were audio-recorded, translated and transcribed.
Transcript data and visual data of 24 photo-essays were analyzed using a
constant comparison approach. We found that participants used alcohol to
build and sustain social networks, meet sexual partners, and enhance
sexual experience. Excessive alcohol use was common, which was
associated with increased HIV risk behaviors within a community of MSM
who maintained multiple partnerships. Our study suggests that HIV
interventions need to address excessive alcohol use to mitigate the
associated HIV risk at both the individual and community levels.},
