@article{WOS:000446353300094,
 abstract = {Floods constitute a major problem that cross geopolitical,
administrative, and sectoral boundaries, and must as such be jointly
governed by a web of actors. The patterns of social relations among
these actors are fundamental for society's capacity to mitigate flood
risk. The purpose of this study is to contribute to our understanding of
flood risk governance by investigating the social organization of formal
actors that contribute to mitigating urban flood risk in Swedish
municipalities. It applies Social Network Analysis to examine what
patterns of dependence, trust, and influence of external actors emerge
in the accounts of politicians and civil servants in Lomma Municipality,
Sweden. The results indicate interesting patterns in type of input, as
well as the role of personal relationships and different forms of
authority for trust and influence. There is also a horizontal decoupling
between municipalities along the river, as well as a vertical decoupling
between the municipal and the national level, where withdrawing national
authorities leave a void increasingly filled by private companies. These
patterns of social relationships between municipal and external actors
contributing to mitigate urban flood risk are important for
understanding flood risk governance in society.},
