@article{WOS:001259000600001,
 abstract = {This study extends pain-driven dual envy theory to explain why emerging
adults who do not attend college might experience uplifting or hostile
reactions to the social media posts of their college-attending peers.
Employing a 2 x 2 experiment (N = 233; Mage = 21.87), we examined how
deservingness perceptions (deserving versus undeserving) and social
approval cues (high versus low) affect the type of envy emerging adults
experience from viewing college success posts on social media. Results
indicated that the more participants perceived the college success of
their peers to be deserved, the more they experienced benign envy.
Conversely, the more participants appraised the college success of their
peers to be undeserved, the more they experienced malicious envy.
Results also indicated that posts with higher social approval indirectly
triggered more pain for participants. Overall, findings help clarify why
social media users can experience both positive and negative effects
from online social comparisons.
College students use social media to highlight their social and
professional successes. We explored how young adults who do not attend
college respond to such posts. We find that college success posts can
lead to two types of envy. When viewers think students earned their
achievements, they tend to experience benign envy. This can lead to
inspiration. When viewers think students did not earn their
achievements, they experience malicious envy. This type of envy is
associated with negative feelings and hostility. We conclude that
college success posts on social media can positively and negatively
affect young adults who do not attend college.},
