@article{WOS:001284594200001,
 abstract = {Two studies examine how experiencing a social need threat (ostracism and
rejection) impacts subsequent preferences for self-disclosure to various
digital audiences. Findings consider how contextual/situational factors
like need threats may impact the appeal of two established perceived
social affordances of media: personalization and privacy/visibility.
Participants took part in a (bogus) social media activity to elicit
feelings of inclusion/ostracization/rejection and then were asked about
sharing their media preferences with various potential audiences.
Results show that social need threats have no significant impact on
privacy preferences but do affect preferences for sharing with some
audiences and not others. Notably, ostracized and rejected participants
show different patterns of preferences, suggesting these forms of social
need threat may have distinct impacts on future self-disclosures.
Implications for online relationship development and community building
are considered in the discussion.
We test how being liked (included), ignored (ostracized), or actively
disliked (rejected) on social media impacts how people want to share
information about themselves. Some research shows that people who are
rejected tend to retreat from social interaction and toward anti-social
behavior. To understand this effect better, we randomly assigned people
to receive a certain number of likes/dislikes in a fake social media
setting, then asked how much participants wanted to stay private (not
share anything), and want to share with various possible audiences, such
as posting only to close friends, an open public, and the group that
ostracized/rejected them. Especially in Study 2, rejected participants
were less interested in continuing to interact with the people from the
social media activity (but this difference was not quite the same for
ostracized people). By contrast, there were no significant differences
in any group's interest in sharing with friends or other audiences, or
their privacy preferences. This preliminary evidence suggests that
rejected people aren't entirely avoiding social interactions but
selectively avoiding the people who hurt them, which may have important
implications for their behavior after this negative experience, both
online and off.},
