@article{WOS:001303280800004,
 abstract = {During the high school period covering adolescence, factors such as exam
stress, adaptation to adolescence, and social media may cause
adolescents to engage in bad eating behaviors and have a negative
attitude towards their own bodies. This experimental study aimed to
evaluate the effect of (short) mindfulness interventions on high school
students eating habits and body image. The sample consisted of a total
of 60 tenth grade students (intervention group n = 30, control group n =
30) studying at a high school in Istanbul. The Information Form, the
Dutch Eating Behavior Questionnaire, the Body Appreciation Scale and the
Body Image Coping Strategies Inventory were used to collect the data.
The in-group comparisons of pretest, posttest, and follow-up scores of
the intervention group indicated an increase in the restrained eating
behavior, body appreciation, and positive rational acceptance scores and
a decrease in the emotional-external eating, appearance fixing, and
avoidance scores (p < 0.001). The inter-group comparisons indicated that
the posttest and follow-up restrained eating, body appreciation,
positive rational acceptance scores of the intervention group were high
(p < 0.05) but that their emotional-external eating, appearance fixing,
and avoidance scores were low compared to the control group (p < 0.05).
It was determined that the mindfulness intervention could be effective
on high school students' eating habits, body appreciation, and body
image coping strategies. It is recommended that technique is used to
prepare teenagers to the changes occurred in their bodies, to reduce
stress and negative attitudes towards themselves and their bodies, and
ensure that they become aware of themselves.},
