@article{WOS:001346013700002,
 abstract = {This study examined the association between co-occurring Autism Spectrum
Disorder (ASD) and Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)
traits and Internet addiction (IA) among college students. This
cross-sectional study included 399 college students and evaluated their
internet usage, the level of IA, presence of ASD traits, and presence of
ADHD traits. The IA levels were measured using Young's Internet
Addiction Test (IAT). The presence of ASD and ADHD traits was measured
using the Adult Autism Spectrum Disorders Self-Rating Scale (A-ASD) and
the Adult ADHD Self-Reporting Scale (ASRS), respectively. The
participants were divided into typical group, ASD traits group, ADHD
traits group, and co-occurring ASD and ADHD traits group based on
participants' scores on the A-ASD and ASRS and the respective cut-off
values. The data collected were analyzed using Kruskal-Wallis one-way
analysis of variance. The group exhibiting co-occurring ASD and ADHD
traits scored significantly higher on the IAT than those in the other
groups, such as the ASD traits group and the ADHD traits group.
Additionally, over one-third of the individuals in the co-occurring ASD
and ADHD traits group spent over three hours daily on social networking
sites (SNSs), compared to approximately one-sixth in the other groups.
The results provide the first findings on the connections between
co-occurring ASD and ADHD traits and IA in college students, which
suggest that ASD and ADHD characteristics may increase the risk of IA,
especially when they are co-occurring.},
