@article{WOS:001463180800001,
 abstract = {The prevalence of adolescent loneliness nearly doubled between 2012 and
2018. Previous research has shown associations between social media and
adolescent mental health. Parenting can affect this relationship, but
this area remains understudied. This study was designed in collaboration
with youth and aimed to understand the association between technology
restrictions and loneliness in adolescents. This secondary analysis used
data from a 2019 survey study among dyads of parents over 17 and
children 13-18 years (n = 4,592). Parents were asked about technology
restrictions, social media use, and demographics. Adolescents were asked
about loneliness, social media use, and parent-adolescent relationship
quality. Linear spline regression with a generalized linear model and
stepwise regression were used to assess the relationship between
technology restrictions and adolescent loneliness while controlling for
covariates of interest. Results conveyed a nonlinear relationship, with
an increase in reported loneliness with the addition of each restriction
between 0 and 2 restrictions and a decrease in loneliness between 2 and
6 restrictions (p<.001). Significant covariates included parent social
media use and parent-adolescent relationship quality. Future studies
should focus on mechanisms behind the association between technology use
and loneliness and the influence of parental factors on adolescent
loneliness. Impact Summary Prior State of Knowledge: Previous research
has established that social media impacts adolescent mental health and
parenting can affect this relationship, but this area remains
understudied. Our study was designed with youth as stakeholders and
experts, an approach which is lacking in extant literature. Novel
Contributions: This manuscript represents a unique approach to
incorporate youth perspectives while also providing them agency as
co-authors. We highlight that parent media use and relationship with
adolescent may play a larger role in adolescent loneliness than their
own media use. Practical Implications: Our findings on the modest
impacts of household media restrictions on adolescent loneliness can be
considered by parents alongside current parental technology mediation
recommendations.},
