@article{WOS:001498284600003,
 abstract = {Co-occurring increases in mental health symptoms and social media use
(SMU) have led researchers to investigate if these phenomena are linked.
However, studies have yielded inconsistent conclusions which indicates
other factors may impact these relationships. Guided by a developmental
psychopathology perspective, we explored if trait negative affect
hypothetically mediated or moderated the associations between social
media use (SMU) and internalizing symptoms in two cohort sequential
samples of diverse, first-year college students in the Southeastern
United States (Cohort 1: N = 191, 69 \% BIPOC, 52 \% female, collected
2021-2022; Cohort 2: N = 195, 81 \% BIPOC, 53 \% female, collected
2022-2023). Further strengths of the design include analysis of multiple
constructs of SMU (frequency/intensity vs. disordered or addictive SMU)
and the structural equation modeling/latent variable approach. Robust
support was demonstrated for indirect effects of negative affect linking
SMU to latent internalizing symptoms across samples and SMU measures.
There was some but less consistent evidence for moderation hypotheses.
Results generally align with a developmental psychopathology framework,
wherein individual differences in negative affect help explain links
between SMU and internalizing symptoms at a sensitive turning point in
emerging adulthood.},
