@article{WOS:001505310900001,
 abstract = {Digital technology has revolutionised the tourism industry, empowering
tourists through information and communication technologies and social
media. This study employs systems theory with a technology acceptance
model to investigate whether the digital empowerment of tourists serves
as a ``threat{''} or a ``treat{''} to the tourism industry. A concurrent
mixed-method strategy, including a survey and a set of in-depth
interviews, is employed. The findings indicate that emergent properties
of digitally empowered tourism are activating competitive dynamics to
shift in control to the tourism regulators which eventually leads
vulnerable bystander effect on tourism businesses. The findings deepened
the understanding of the dynamics of tourists' digital empowerment and
its implications for regulators and businesses. The findings help
tourists to comprehend the new concept of digital empowerment,
regulators to design effective and relevant regulatory frameworks, and
tourism businesses to implement novel methods that allow them to provide
tourism offerings to attract modern tourists.},
