@article{WOS:001574419600001,
 abstract = {PurposeThis study aimed to examine the relationship between body mass
index (BMI), the social media use disorder scale (SMUDS) and scale of
effects of social media on eating behaviour (SESMEB) and to test the
mindful eating questionnaire (MEQ) as a potential
mediator.Design/methodology/approachIn this cross-sectional study, 2,296
(45.0\%) males and 2,804 (55.0\%) females, 5,100 adults in total,
between 18 and 64 years old through an online survey via social media
platforms. Participants were mostly single (69.9\%), undergraduates
(78.1\%), and living in urban areas (78.9\%). IBM-AMOS was used to
explore whether mindful eating mediated the associations between social
media parameters and BMI.FindingsThe structural equation modeling (SEM)
showed that enforcement affects five sub-dimensions of mindful eating,
and escape affects emotional eating and interference. Operation and
escape dimensions of SMUDS had an indirect effect by affecting mindful
eating, 3.1\% of the total effect on BMI change. The direct effect of
MEQ and SMUDS scores on BMI was found to be statistically significant
(beta = -0.196 and -0.152, respectively), but the relationship between
SESMEB and BMI was not significant. SMUDS indirectly affected 21.4\% of
the total effect in BMI change, contributing to the development of the
MEQ.Originality/valueSocial media use disorder has been associated with
increased BMI and unhealthy eating behaviors. The present study revealed
that social media use disorder is associated with increased BMI, and
increased mindful eating might play a protective role against obesity.
On the other hand, it is important to recognize that obesity is a
complex biopsychosocial phenomenon influenced by multiple risk factors,
and the underlying processes are highly intricate.},
