@article{WOS:001576998300001,
 abstract = {PurposeThe main purpose of this study is to examine the perceived
self-media distortion's direct and indirect effects on police job stress
through behavioral adjustments.Design/methodology/approachUsing survey
data collected from 1,900 police officers in a northern Chinese
province, we tested a moderated mediation model featuring the moderating
effect of perceived self-legitimacy on the relationships between
perceived self-media distortion and stress.FindingsWe found that
perceived self-media distortion is directly and indirectly connected to
officers' stress via adjusting behaviors. In addition, the positive
relationship between perceived distortion and adjustment is weaker among
officers with greater perception of self-legitimacy, whereas the
positive association between adjustment and stress is stronger among
those with higher levels of perceived self-legitimacy.Research
limitations/implicationsOur study relied on a convenience sample of
officers from a single Chinese province. We also lacked official data on
police officers in the two cities examined within the province and were
unable to accurately assess the representativeness of our sample. More
officer-centered stress relief initiatives focusing on reducing
media-induced stress should be developed and implemented. Officers
should be encouraged to practice self-care activities, curate their
social media feeds and practice mindfulness while consuming media and
managing their exposure to potentially stressful
content.Originality/valueThis study extends recent research on the
impact of negative publicity on the police by further assessing
officers' experiences with behavioral adjustments and psychological
stress simultaneously. It also expands the research on officer
psychological readiness by investigating whether self-legitimacy (e.g.
self-recognition of their entitlement to the legal authority) can
moderate the associations between media distortion, behavioral
adjustments and job stress.},
