@article{WOS:A1993KU28600004,
 abstract = {Building on several recent investigations, we propose a theoretical
model in which the effects on loneliness of an individual's personality
characteristics and features of his or her social network are mediated
by the availability of specific social provisions. More than 500 college
students completed questionnaires assessing personality variables,
social network characteristics, structural features of the social
environment, and three kinds of loneliness (global, social, and
emotional). In keeping with the model, personality and social network
features were related significantly and substantially to the
availability of social provisions; provisions, in turn, were associated
substantially with all three kinds of loneliness. Moreover, the direct
effects of personality and network features on loneliness were explained
largely by social provisions. In most cases, controlling statistically
for provisions reduced the size of these direct effects by at least 75
to 85 percent. We discuss the implications of these results,
psychometric problems in the social provision measures, and future
directions for research.},
